title: Local salvage for prostate cancer recurrence after radiation
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Who benefits:'' Consider potentially curative salvage for [[biopsy-proven local recurrence|Work-up of prostate cancer recurrence after radiation]], no distant metastases, adequate life expectancy, and acceptable urinary function. Favor lower PSA, longer disease-free interval, and limited local extent; review prior radiation dose/fields and residual toxicity in a multidisciplinary team.
* ''Options:'' Salvage radical prostatectomy with pelvic lymph-node dissection; reirradiation with low-/high-dose-rate brachytherapy or stereotactic body radiotherapy; cryotherapy; or high-intensity focused ultrasound (HIFU). No modality has established comparative superiority; use experienced centers and trials when available.
* ''Reirradiation selection:'' NCCN particularly supports consideration when >2 years have elapsed since radiation and no ongoing moderate-or-worse radiation toxicity exists. Treatment volume is individualized; focal salvage remains under investigation.
* ''Counsel:'' All approaches risk urinary and sexual morbidity. Prostatectomy particularly risks incontinence, erectile dysfunction, and anastomotic stricture; ablation/reirradiation can cause obstruction, rectal injury, or fistula. Baseline function matters.
* ''Evidence limits:'' NCCN lists irreversible electroporation as category 2B. EAU restricts cryotherapy/HIFU to trials or prospective cohorts. ADT is not automatic for prostate-only salvage; prospective evidence does not define its optimal use/duration with reirradiation.
* ''Regional nodes:'' Pelvic nodal recurrence after prostate radiation without prior pelvic nodal irradiation: offer pelvic nodal radiation plus ADT. Previously irradiated nodes require individualized planning; consider [[systemic options|Monitoring and systemic treatment of prostate cancer recurrence after radiation]].

Sources: NCCN Prostate Cancer v7.2026, PROS-L 1/2, MS-36; ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 14, p. 758 (PDF 765); [[AUA/ASTRO/SUO 2024|https://www.auanet.org/documents/Guidelines/PDF/2024%20Guidelines/STPC%20Unabridged%20Final.pdf]], statements 24/27; [[EAU 2026|https://uroweb.org/guidelines/prostate-cancer/chapter/treatment]], §6.4.7.
